# Reflexion

A **Reflexion** agent closes the loop on its own work: **generate → critique →
revise**, repeated, with the whole trace kept as **memory** so each pass sees what
the last one tried and complained about. Memory here is a chain of *self-judgments*.

defmodule Reflection do
  defstruct pass: 0, score: 0, verdict: :reject, gripes: []
end

@verdicts [:accept, :revise, :reject]

The critic grades its **own** draft against an explicit rubric — each line a yes/no
with a reason, which keeps the self-evaluation honest instead of a vague "8/10":

agent :reflexion do
  @rubric [{:personalized, 30}, {:concrete_value, 30}, {:short, 20}, {:clear_cta, 20}]

  def critique(%Draft{} = draft) do
    judged = Enum.map(@rubric, fn {dim, weight} -> {dim, weight, passes?(draft, dim)} end)
    score  = Enum.sum(for {_, w, true} <- judged, do: w)
    %Reflection{pass: draft.pass, score: score,
                gripes: (for {dim, _, false} <- judged, do: dim),
                verdict: if(score >= 85, do: :accept, else: :revise)}
  end

  # generate → critique → recurse until accepted or the pass budget runs out
  def refine(lead, brief, passes \\ 4), do: do_refine(lead, brief, [], passes)

  defp do_refine(_lead, _brief, [last | _] = trace, 0), do: {:exhausted, last, trace}
  defp do_refine(lead, brief, trace, n) do
    draft = generate(lead, brief, trace)
    case critique(draft) do
      %Reflection{verdict: :accept} = r -> {:ok, draft, [r | trace]}
      r -> do_refine(lead, brief, [r | trace], n - 1)
    end
  end
end

`max_passes` is a safety net, not a quality gate — the loop stops when the work is
good. Because the trace is typed `%Reflection{}` data, `work why` can show *why* an
email reads the way it does. Next: the [[Plan]] board.
